<Docs slug="terminals">
  <h1>Terminals</h1>
  <p class="lead">The sidebar lists every project's tasks and terminals. Pick one and it fills the window.</p>

  <h2>Sidebar</h2>
  <p>
    Under <strong>Projects</strong>, each project lists its
    tasks in board order: those with terminals open, and those in progress or in review without one, marked
    with a moon until you open one. Its shells follow. A task running several terminals shows each one
    under it. Click a project to go back to the board or terminals you last had open there, even after a restart, or click its arrow to fold it away; dots after its name show what is
    still working or finished unseen. An agent that finished while you were looking elsewhere shows four solid
    squares in the accent color until you open it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The buttons on a project's row open its board and make a new task in it; its <strong>…</strong> menu, also
    on right-click, opens its settings, pull requests and analysis, and a new terminal. Right-click a task for the same menu the board gives it.
    Switch to <strong>Tags</strong> to list tasks under their tags instead, with untagged ones under
    <strong>No tag</strong>. Hide the sidebar with the button at its top; hovering the left edge brings it
    back.
  </p>

  <h2>Card stack</h2>
  <p>
    Each terminal runs your default shell in the task's worktree. A task's terminals stack, the active one in
    front and the rest peeking out behind it; click a background card to bring it forward. Cards stay in the
    order you last used them, so the one you just left is directly behind the one in front. A shell is a card
    of its own. When an agent runs in a card, its status (thinking or idle) shows on the card even when it is
    behind others. See <a href="/docs/harnesses/">Harnesses</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Action</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>⌘K</code> / <code>Ctrl+K</code></td><td>Open the <a href="/docs/palette/">command palette</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘I</code> / <code>Ctrl+I</code></td><td>New terminal in the project on screen</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘W</code> / <code>Ctrl+W</code></td><td>Close terminal</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘1</code>-<code>⌘9</code> / <code>Ctrl+1</code>-<code>Ctrl+9</code></td><td>Select a card in the stack by position</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘T</code> / <code>Ctrl+T</code></td><td>Open the project's board</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘N</code> / <code>Ctrl+N</code></td><td>New task</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘P</code> / <code>Ctrl+P</code></td><td>Open the runner <a href="/docs/panels/">panel</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘D</code> / <code>Ctrl+D</code></td><td>Toggle the <a href="/docs/diff/">diff panel</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    With nothing open, the window offers to <a href="/docs/resume/">resume your last session</a> and lists
    recent tasks so you can pick up where you left off.
  </p>

  <h2>Tag terminals</h2>
  <p>
    Tag a terminal from the tag button on its card. Each terminal keeps its own tag, including across a restart,
    so two terminals of one task can carry different ones. The sidebar's <strong>Tags</strong> view groups what's
    running by them.
  </p>
  <p>Manage tags from the shell or an agent with <a href="/docs/cli/"><code>ouijit tag</code></a>.</p>
</Docs>
